billNumber":"88","title":"Providing congressional disapproval under chapter 8 of title 5, United States Code, of the rule submitted by the Environmental Protection Agency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine Pollution Control Standards; Advanced Clean Cars II; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-16.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Administrative law and regulatory procedures","Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Congressional oversight","Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)","Federal preemption","Hybrid, electric, and advanced technology vehicles","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000302","cosponsorCount":41,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/88","industries":[{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"Automotive manufacturers face different emission and zero-emission vehicle requirements depending on whether California's waiver stands or is revoked."},{"industry":"energy-fossil","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"Zero-emission vehicle mandates directly affect demand for gasoline and diesel fuel in California's large vehicle market."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Electric vehicle adoption rates influence demand for charging infrastructure and electricity from renewable sources."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.6,"rationale":"The pace of electric vehicle deployment affects electricity demand and grid infrastructure investment needs."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel a federal waiver that allows California to enforce its Advanced Clean Cars II regulations. Those regulations set stricter standards for vehicle emissions and require automakers to sell more zero-emission vehicles in California. If the resolution passes, California woul